![](/images/articles/cat-in-the-hat-and-musicians.jpeg)

Nothing has made me a strident feminist more than having a daughter. I’m acutely aware that the narrator of The Cat in the Hat is male. (The fish gets to talk. Shouldn’t Sally ought to have gotten a speaking part?)

> I’m acutely aware that the narrator of The Cat in the Hat is male.

This morning I was Mr. Mom. I entertained Maya with three music videos on my iPad. Without choosing too carefully, I played All About that Bass (Meghan Trainor), Roar (Katie Perry), and Shake it Off (Taylor Swift). I wasn’t searching for the songs… just clicking on successive YouTube recommendations.

I got to thinking about female artists from my teens and 20s. Madonna. Cyndi Lauper. Alanis.

They all had some good songs, but I don’t love their lyrics for my kid. Material Girl. Girls Just Wanna Have Fun. You Oughta Know (“would she go down on you in a theater”)? Do I show my age too much if I think that one of Madonna’s contributions to modern culture was the sexualizing of thirteen year-olds? That Britney Spears (who really should be no one’s role model) is the logical consequence of the Madonna ideal?

Then I considered the songs from my morning. All About that Bass is about being confident in your appearance even if you don’t look like a supermodel. Roar is an expression of power and personal energy. Shake it Off is about not listening too much to what others say about you — shaking off the bullshit. And Taylor Swift seems like she’s a pretty fair role model. (I hope one day I don’t regret writing that.)

Was the music today better? Nope. Do I recognize that Madonna's talent and strength is something to admire, and that she advanced the cause of women artists? Yes, I do.

> I’d rather my daughter listen to the music I heard this morning than to the female artists that were top-of-mind for me 20 and 30 years ago.

But all in all, I’d rather my daughter listen to the music I heard this morning than to the female artists that were top-of-mind for me 20 and 30 years ago. And that’s not a bad way to start a weekend.
